# The binary `go build` leaves next to the source, whatever it is called.
#
# An allow-list, not a name: a named rule protects against the binary you
# already met, and a rename produces exactly one artefact nobody has a rule
# for. (In go-filesystems/smb a 6.5 MB binary was committed by the very change
# that renamed the command, because the new rule named the NEW name.)
/fileshare
/fileshare.exe
/cover.out

# ⛔ `.exe` is on that list because a `GOOS=windows go build ./...` left one
# here and `git add -A` staged it -- the same defect, in its fourth variant,
# and caught only because the WHOLE of `git status` was read rather than its
# first few lines. A rule that names one artefact is a rule that has met one
# artefact.
